{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Rudaz","name":"Rudaz, Damien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Broth","name":"Broth, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Mlynar","name":"Mlynar, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Everything Counts: The Managed Omnirelevance of Speech in Human-Voice Agent Interaction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T10:12:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:57:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-18T17:34:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T01:17:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3820655","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To this day, turn-taking models determining voice agents' conduct have been examined primarily from a technical point of view, while the ways in which they emerge as interactional constraints or resources for human conversationalists in situ remain underexplored. Drawing on a detailed analysis of corpora of naturalistic data, we document how humans' conduct was produced in reference to the ever-present risk that, each time they spoke, their talk might trigger a new uncalled-for contribution from the artificial agent. We examine this phenomenon in interactions involving rule-based robots from a 'pre-LLM era' as well as the most recent voice agents. This 'omnirelevance of human speech' (i.e., the possibility that a conversational agent may erroneously respond to any speech it detects) emerged as a constitutive feature of these human-agent encounters. We describe some of the practices through which humans managed these artificial agents' turn-taking conduct. Given recent improvements in voice capture technology, we ask whether this 'omnirelevance of human speech' weighs even more heavily on human practices today than in the past."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-12T02:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yangqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenPhone: Mobile Agentic Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T20:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:12:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T17:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T01:17:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:47:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the advancement of multimodal large language models (MLLMs), building GUI agent systems has become an increasingly promising direction--especially for mobile platforms, given their rich app ecosystems and intuitive touch interactions. Yet mobile GUI agents face a critical dilemma: truly on-device models (4B or smaller) lack sufficient performance, while capable models (starting from 7B) are either too large for mobile deployment or prohibitively costly (e.g., cloud-only closed-source MLLMs). To resolve this, we propose OpenPhone, a mobile GUI agent system that leverages device-cloud collaboration to tap the cost-efficiency of on device models and the high capability of cloud models, while avoiding their drawbacks. Specifically, OpenPhone enhances Qwen2.5-VL-3B via two-stage SFT-\u0026gt;GRPO training on synthetic GUI data for strong decision-making, integrates an efficient long-reasoning and memory management mechanism to utilize historical interactions under tight resources, and defaults to on-device execution--only escalating challenging subtasks to the cloud via real-time complexity assessment. Experiments on the online AndroidLab benchmark and diverse apps show OpenPhone matches or nears larger models, with a significant reduction in cloud costs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22422","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22422","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22422","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel","familyName":"Flint","name":"Flint, Ariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca Maria","familyName":"Aiello","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romualdo","familyName":"Pastor-Satorras","name":"Pastor-Satorras, Romualdo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Baronchelli","name":"Baronchelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group size effects and collective misalignment in LLM multi-agent systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T19:45:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T01:06:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1073/pnas.2531697123","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent systems of large language models (LLMs) are rapidly expanding across domains, introducing dynamics not captured by single-agent evaluations. Yet, existing work has mostly contrasted the behavior of a single agent with that of a collective of fixed size, leaving open a central question: how does group size shape dynamics? Here, we move beyond this dichotomy and systematically explore outcomes across the full range of group sizes. We focus on multi-agent misalignment, building on recent evidence that interacting LLMs playing a simple coordination game can generate collective biases absent in individual models. First, we show that collective bias is a deeper phenomenon than previously assessed: interaction can amplify individual biases, introduce new ones, or override model-level preferences. Second, we demonstrate that group size affects the dynamics in a non-linear way, revealing model-dependent dynamical regimes. Finally, we develop a mean-field analytical approach and show that, above a critical population size, simulations converge to deterministic predictions that expose the basins of attraction of competing equilibria. These findings establish group size as a key driver of multi-agent dynamics and highlight the need to consider population-level effects when deploying LLM-based systems at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22422","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:00Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:47:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22775","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22775","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22775","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjielong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Junjielong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Boyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinjia","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pinjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Supervision for Software Agents via Patch Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:52:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T11:43:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:43:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While language model agents have advanced software engineering, existing test-based supervision is limiting its scalability on real-world issues. The reason is twofold: (1) high-coverage tests are naturally rare in the wild, and (2) building and running test sandbox is heavy and fragile. To unlock supervision scaling, we propose R4P, a reasoning-based method that provides scaffold-agnostic rewards. R4P uses a group-wise training objective, enabling it to verify multiple patches against each other's modification and gain a dense reward for supervising agents without executing tests or relying on specific agent trajectories. R4P achieves 72.2% Acc. for verifying patches from SWE-bench, competitive with proprietary models. To show the downstream practical utility of R4P, we design and train an execution-free scaffold, Mini-SE, with pure RL via R4P. Mini-SE achieves 26.2% Pass@1, showing a 10.0% improvement over the original Qwen3-32B, and can be further improved to 32.8% with R4P for test-time scaling on patch selection. The stable scaling curves illustrate that though imperfect, R4P can still reliably support downstream tasks at scale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP'26 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22775","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:19Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:23:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijit","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Abhijit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niraj K.","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Niraj K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan D.","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Jonathan D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas L.","familyName":"Griffiths","name":"Griffiths, Thomas L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hongjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana","familyName":"Marculescu","name":"Marculescu, Diana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashiqur","familyName":"Rasul","name":"Rasul, Ashiqur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshab K.","familyName":"Parhi","name":"Parhi, Keshab K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Vision Toward Energy-Efficient Domain-Specific Artificial Intelligence Models and Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T22:21:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-09T14:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T00:45:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of artificial intelligence (AI) has taken a tight hold on broad aspects of society, industry, business, and governance in ways that dictate the prosperity and might of the world's economies. The AI market size is projected to grow from {\\$}189 billion in 2023 to {\\$}4.8 trillion by 2033. Currently, AI is dominated by large language models (LLMs) that exhibit linguistic and visual intelligence. However, training these models requires a massive amount of data scraped from the web as well as large amounts of energy (50-60 GWh to train GPT-4). Despite these costs, these models often hallucinate, a characteristic that prevents them from being deployed in critical application domains. In contrast, the human brain consumes only 20W of power. What is needed is the next level of AI evolution in which lightweight domain-specific multimodal models, especially compact models with 10--20B parameters for bounded domains, with higher levels of intelligence can reason, plan, and make decisions in dynamic environments with real-time data and prior knowledge, while learning continuously and evolving in ways that enhance future decision-making capability. This will define the next wave of AI, progressing from today's large models, trained with vast amounts of data, to nimble energy-efficient domain-specific agents that can reason and think in a world full of uncertainty. To support such agents, hardware will need to be reimagined to allow system-level energy efficiencies $\\geq {1000X}$ over the state of the art for targeted domain tasks, subject to accuracy, latency, and coverage constraints. Such a vision of future AI systems is developed in this work."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:06:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-10T01:55:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zesen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zesen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuchong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongdong","familyName":"She","name":"She, Dongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Compression Becomes an Attack Surface: Black-Box Attacks on Prompt-Compressed LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T09:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:28:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T08:45:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-19T20:25:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:07:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt compression is increasingly deployed in LLM agents to reduce latency and cost, but it also determines what the backend LLM ultimately sees. We show that, when trusted and untrusted inputs are compressed under a shared budget, this lossy transformation creates a new attack surface: by perturbing only untrusted inputs before compression, an adversary can cause the compressor to discard task-critical evidence or safety guardrails before inference. Unlike prompt injection, jailbreaks, or RAG poisoning, the attack target is the compressor rather than the backend LLM; the perturbation need not encode a meaningful instruction or survive compression. We formalize this vulnerability as adversarial information loss (AIL), the excess downstream distortion caused by adversarially steering a lossy compressor beyond benign compression alone. To exploit AIL, we present COMA, a transfer-based black-box attack that optimizes pre-compression perturbations using attacker-side surrogate compressors and backend LLMs. Across three tasks and six compressors, COMA achieves 0.71 average ASR, versus 0.21 for the strongest baseline, and transfers to two real-world agent case studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:35:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22768","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav Narayanan","familyName":"Venkit","name":"Venkit, Pranav Narayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Believing? Evaluating Vision-Language Model Susceptibility in Agent-to-Agent Multimodal Persuasion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T23:23:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As autonomous agents increasingly interact, they inevitably attempt to influence one another. While prior work in text-only settings has explored the dynamics of Agent-to-Agent (A2A) persuasion, the rise of Vision-Language Models (VLMs) introduces a more complex challenge: multimodal content conveys richer information while integrating subtle, hard-to-detect persuasive cues. To study this vulnerability, we present MMPersuade, a unified framework and dataset for A2A multimodal persuasion. We model interactions between a persuader agent, which leverages images and psychological strategies, and a persuadee VLM. Our benchmark spans commercial, subjective and behavioral, and adversarial contexts, and evaluates persuasion via function-calling that capture behavioral shifts beyond verbal responses. Experiments on six VLMs reveal three findings: (1) multimodal inputs consistently outperform text-only persuasion, with raw visual signals uniquely increasing susceptibility in adversarial settings by bypassing text-activated safety defenses; (2) persuadee vulnerability is highly domain- and format-dependent, with realistic and community-style formats driving susceptibility in commercial settings while different formats dominate in adversarial ones; and (3) psychological strategy efficacy varies with context and model architecture, as more capable models resist benign persuasion yet become more susceptible under adversarial multimodal inputs. Our framework provides a foundation for building more robust and aligned VLMs in multi-agent environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22768","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:08Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Rorseth","name":"Rorseth, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parke","familyName":"Godfrey","name":"Godfrey, Parke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukasz","familyName":"Golab","name":"Golab, Lukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divesh","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Divesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jarek","familyName":"Szlichta","name":"Szlichta, Jarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rule-Based Explanations for Retrieval-Augmented LLM Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:22:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T22:30:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:21:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"If-then rules are widely used to explain machine learning models; e.g., \"if employed = no, then loan application = rejected.\" We present the first proposal to apply rules to explain the emerging class of large language models (LLMs) with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Since RAG enables LLM systems to incorporate retrieved information sources at inference time, rules linking the presence or absence of sources can explain output provenance; e.g., \"if a Times Higher Education ranking article is retrieved, then the LLM ranks Oxford first.\" To generate such rules, a brute force approach would probe the LLM with all source combinations and check if the presence or absence of any sources leads to the same output. We propose optimizations to speed up rule generation, inspired by Apriori-like pruning from frequent itemset mining but redefined within the scope of our novel problem. We conclude with qualitative and quantitative experiments demonstrating our solutions' value and efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:17Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:38:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gwihwan","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Gwihwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chijin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LSPRAG: LSP-Guided RAG for Language-Agnostic Real-Time Unit Test Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T08:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:29:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T03:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3744916.3773189","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated unit test generation is essential for robust software development, yet existing approaches struggle to generalize across multiple programming languages and operate within real-time development. While Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a promising solution, their ability to generate high coverage test code depends on prompting a concise context of the focal method. Current solutions, such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation, either rely on imprecise similarity-based searches or demand the creation of costly, language-specific static analysis pipelines. To address this gap, we present LSPRAG, a framework for concise-context retrieval tailored for real-time, language-agnostic unit test generation. LSPRAG leverages off-the-shelf Language Server Protocol (LSP) back-ends to supply LLMs with precise symbol definitions and references in real time. By reusing mature LSP servers, LSPRAG provides an LLM with language-aware context retrieval, requiring minimal per-language engineering effort. We evaluated LSPRAG on open-source projects spanning Java, Go, and Python. Compared to the best performance of baselines, LSPRAG increased line coverage by up to 174.55% for Golang, 213.31% for Java, and 31.57% for Python."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:09:55Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:09:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yizhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Boyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyang","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengliang","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Chengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimin","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Shimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ju","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fugee","familyName":"Tsung","name":"Tsung, Fugee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiannan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenglin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chenglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaolei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanhe","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xuanhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoliang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of Data Agents: Emerging Paradigm or Overstated Hype?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:54:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T15:16:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:54:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has spurred the emergence of data agents, autonomous systems designed to orchestrate Data + AI ecosystems for tackling complex data-related tasks. However, the term \"data agent\" currently suffers from terminological ambiguity and inconsistent adoption, conflating simple query responders with sophisticated autonomous architectures. This terminological ambiguity fosters mismatched user expectations, accountability challenges, and barriers to industry growth. Inspired by the SAE J3016 standard for driving automation, this survey introduces the first systematic hierarchical taxonomy for data agents, comprising six levels that delineate and trace progressive shifts in autonomy, from manual operations (L0) to a vision of generative, fully autonomous data agents (L5), thereby clarifying capability boundaries and responsibility allocation. Through this lens, we offer a structured review of existing research arranged by increasing autonomy, encompassing specialized data agents for data management, preparation, and analysis, alongside emerging efforts toward versatile, comprehensive systems with enhanced autonomy. We further analyze critical evolutionary leaps and technical gaps for advancing data agents, especially the ongoing L2-to-L3 transition, where data agents evolve from procedural execution to autonomous orchestration. Finally, we conclude with a forward-looking roadmap, envisioning proactive, generative data agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Please refer to our paper list and companion materials at: https://github.com/HKUSTDial/awesome-data-agents"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:40Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:10:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Bazinska","name":"Bazinska, Julia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Mathys","name":"Mathys, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Casucci","name":"Casucci, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mateo","familyName":"Rojas-Carulla","name":"Rojas-Carulla, Mateo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xander","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Xander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Souly","name":"Souly, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking Agent Backbones: Evaluating the Security of Backbone LLMs in AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T10:36:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:57:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T09:00:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents powered by large language models (LLMs) are being deployed at scale, yet we lack a systematic understanding of how the choice of backbone LLM affects agent security. The non-deterministic sequential nature of AI agents complicates security modeling, while the integration of traditional software with AI components entangles novel LLM vulnerabilities with conventional security risks. Existing frameworks only partially address these challenges as they either capture specific vulnerabilities only or require modeling of complete agents. To address these limitations, we introduce threat snapshots: a framework that isolates specific states in an agent's execution flow where LLM vulnerabilities manifest, enabling the systematic identification and categorization of security risks that propagate from the LLM to the agent level. We apply this framework to construct the $b^3$ benchmark, a security benchmark based on 194,331 unique crowdsourced adversarial attacks. We then evaluate 34 popular LLMs with it, revealing, among other insights, that enhanced reasoning capabilities improve security, while model size does not correlate with security. We release our benchmark, dataset, and evaluation code to facilitate widespread adoption by LLM providers and practitioners, offering guidance for agent developers and incentivizing model developers to prioritize backbone security improvements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Julia Bazinska and Max Mathys contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:38Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:10:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carl","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Carl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Agentic Reasoning in LLM Judges via Tool-Integrated Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:23:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-21T05:25:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely used as judges to evaluate response quality, providing a scalable alternative to human evaluation. However, most LLM judges operate solely on intrinsic text-based reasoning, limiting their ability to verify complex constraints or perform accurate computation. Motivated by the success of tool-integrated reasoning (TIR) in numerous tasks, we propose TIR-Judge, an end-to-end RL framework for training LLM judges that integrates a code executor for precise evaluation. TIR-Judge is built on three principles: (i) diverse training across verifiable and non-verifiable domains, (ii) flexible judgment formats (pointwise, pairwise, listwise), and (iii) iterative RL that bootstraps directly from the initial model without distillation. On seven public benchmarks, TIR-Judge surpasses strong reasoning-based judges by up to 6.4% (pointwise) and 7.7% (pairwise), and achieves listwise performance comparable to Claude-Opus-4 despite having only 8B parameters. Remarkably, TIR-Judge-Zero - trained entirely without distilled judge trajectories, matches the performance of distilled variants, demonstrating that tool-augmented judges can self-evolve through iterative reinforcement learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roham","familyName":"Koohestani","name":"Koohestani, Roham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Podkopaev","name":"Podkopaev, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maliheh","familyName":"Izadi","name":"Izadi, Maliheh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Agents Probabilistic Automata? A Trace-Based, Memory-Constrained Theory of Agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Formal Languages and Automata Theory (cs.FL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:22:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:53:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T10:33:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:42:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper studies standard controller architectures for agentic AI and derives automata-theoretic models of their interaction behavior via trace semantics and abstraction. We model an agent implementation as a finite control program augmented with explicit memory primitives (bounded buffers, a call stack, or read/write external memory) and a stochastic policy component (e.g., an LLM) that selects among architecturally permitted actions. Instead of equating the concrete agent with a deterministic acceptor, we treat the agent-environment closed loop as inducing a probability distribution over finite interaction traces. Given an abstraction function $\\Abs$ from concrete configurations to a finite abstract state space, we obtain a probabilistic trace language and an abstract probabilistic transition model $M_{\\Abs}$ suitable for probabilistic model checking.\n Imposing explicit, framework-auditable restrictions on memory access and control flow, we prove that the support of the resulting trace language is regular for bounded-memory controllers, context-free for strict call-return controllers, and recursively enumerable for controllers equipped with unbounded read/write memory. These correspondences allow the reuse of existing verification methods for finite-state and pushdown systems, and they delineate precisely when undecidability barriers arise. The probabilistic semantics leads to quantitative analyses such as: what is the probability of entering an unsafe abstract region, and how can we bound this probability in the presence of environment nondeterminism."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:15Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:26:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming-Tung","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Ming-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuh-Jzer","familyName":"Joung","name":"Joung, Yuh-Jzer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TALM: Dynamic Tree-Structured Multi-Agent Framework with Long-Term Memory for Scalable Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T05:07:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:22:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T15:04:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:27:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic code generation requires large language models (LLMs) capable of complex context management and multi-step reasoning. Prior multi-agent frameworks attempt to address these challenges through collaboration, yet they often suffer from rigid workflows and high reasoning recovery costs. To overcome these limitations, we propose TALM (Tree-Structured Multi-Agent Framework with Long-Term Memory), a dynamic framework that integrates structured task decomposition, localized re-reasoning, and long-term memory mechanisms. TALM employs an extensible tree-based collaboration structure. The parent-child relationships, when combined with a divide-and-conquer strategy, enhance reasoning flexibility and enable efficient error correction across diverse task scopes. Furthermore, a long-term memory module enables semantic querying and integration of prior knowledge, supporting implicit self-improvement through experience reuse. Experimental results on HumanEval, BigCodeBench, and ClassEval benchmarks demonstrate that TALM consistently delivers strong reasoning performance and high token efficiency, highlighting its robustness and practical utility in complex code generation tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:51Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiali","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjishnu","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anjishnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshan","familyName":"Lal","name":"Lal, Roshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Rajasekaran","name":"Rajasekaran, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hani","familyName":"Ramezani","name":"Ramezani, Hani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar Zia","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Omar Zia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleg","familyName":"Rokhlenko","name":"Rokhlenko, Oleg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunny","familyName":"Chiu-Webster","name":"Chiu-Webster, Sunny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Amiri","name":"Amiri, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebATLAS: An LLM Agent with Experience-Driven Memory and Action Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T16:03:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-19T23:16:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:09:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) web agents often struggle with long-horizon web navigation and web task completion in new websites, producing inefficient action sequences unless fine-tuned on environment-specific data. We show that experience-driven memory, combined with look-ahead action simulation, is sufficient for LLM agents to adapt to unseen web environments by remembering past failures and predicting the consequences of future actions. We introduce WebATLAS (Actor-Critic Task-completion with Look-ahead Action Simulation), a memory-augmented LLM web agent that learns a lightweight internal model of the environment from interaction experience and performs hypothetical action rollouts before acting in the real world. WebATLAS builds a persistent cognitive map via curiosity-driven exploration, stores interaction outcomes as experience-based memory, and evaluates candidate actions in cognitive space using a planner--simulator--critic loop. This enables the agent to reuse past experience, avoid previously unsuccessful behaviors, and generate more efficient plans. We evaluate WebATLAS on the WebArena-Lite benchmark for autonomous web navigation and demonstrate a success rate of 63%, outperforming the previous state-of-the-art at 53.9%. Unlike previous systems, our modular architecture requires no website-specific LLM fine-tuning. Ablation studies confirm that experience-driven memory, look-ahead action simulation, and hierarchical replanning play complementary roles in enabling robust, training-free web agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Language Agents and World Models"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:17Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:25:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yunshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Meta-Orchestrator for Multi-task Copilots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T18:13:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T05:01:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Microsoft Copilot suites serve as the universal entry point for various agents skilled in handling important tasks, ranging from assisting a customer with product purchases to detecting vulnerabilities in corporate programming code. Each agent can be powered by language models, software engineering operations, such as database retrieval, and internal \\\u0026amp; external knowledge. The repertoire of a copilot can expand dynamically with new agents. This requires a robust orchestrator that can distribute tasks from user prompts to the right agents. In this work, we propose an Agentic Meta-orchestrator (AMO) for handling multiple tasks and scalable agents in copilot services, which can provide both natural language and action responses. We will also demonstrate the planning that leverages meta-learning, i.e., a trained decision tree model for deciding the best inference strategy among various agents/models. We showcase the effectiveness of our AMO through two production use cases: Microsoft 365 (M365) E-Commerce Copilot and code compliance copilot. M365 E-Commerce Copilot advertises Microsoft products to external customers to promote sales success. The M365 E-Commerce Copilot provides up-to-date product information and connects to multiple agents, such as relational databases and human customer support. The code compliance copilot scans the internal DevOps code to detect known and new compliance issues in pull requests (PR)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:27Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:16:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21908","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21908","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21908","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enabling Robust In-Context Memory and Rapid Task Adaptation in Transformers with Hebbian and Gradient-Based Plasticity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:26:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:05:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T22:10:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models display in-context learning as an emergent effect of scale, but they rely on static weights during inference. In contrast, biological systems continually adapt via synaptic plasticity. We investigate whether explicit, biologically inspired plasticity can endow Transformers with faster in-sequence adaptation. To this end, we augment decoder-only Transformers with fast-weight modules updated either by (i) a neuromodulated Hebbian rule or (ii) the gradient-based plasticity mechanism of Duan et al. (2023). Across copying, regression, and few-shot classification tasks (CIFAR-FS, Omniglot), Hebbian plasticity consistently achieves lower loss and stronger few-shot generalization, while gradient-based updates perform best on long-horizon credit assignment. When associations are short and linearly separable, static weights suffice, defining a clear boundary condition for when plasticity helps. Analysis of learned modulatory signals reveals that gradient-based rules maintain large, persistent updates, whereas Hebbian plasticity is sharply gated around salient events. Together, these results show that explicit plasticity complements attention by enabling rapid, task-specific adaptation, and clarify when different plasticity mechanisms are most effective."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21908","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:16:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Muhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostofa","familyName":"Patwary","name":"Patwary, Mostofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"You","name":"You, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Evolve: LLM Self-Improve through Co-evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T03:30:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:27:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T04:45:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) has demonstrated significant potential in enhancing the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). However, the success of RL for LLMs heavily relies on human-curated datasets and verifiable rewards, which limit their scalability and generality. Recent Self-Play RL methods, inspired by the success of the paradigm in games and Go, aim to enhance LLM reasoning capabilities without human-annotated data. However, their methods primarily depend on a grounded environment for feedback (e.g., a Python interpreter or a game engine); extending them to general domains remains challenging. To address these challenges, we propose Multi-Agent Evolve (MAE), a framework that enables LLMs to self-evolve in solving diverse tasks, including mathematics, reasoning, and general knowledge Q\u0026amp;A. The core design of MAE is based on a triplet of interacting agents (Proposer, Solver, Judge) that are instantiated from a single LLM, and applies reinforcement learning to optimize their behaviors. The Proposer generates questions, the Solver attempts solutions, and the Judge evaluates both while co-evolving. Experiments on Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct demonstrate that MAE achieves an average improvement of 4.54% on multiple benchmarks. These results highlight MAE as a scalable, data-efficient method for enhancing the general reasoning abilities of LLMs with minimal reliance on human-curated supervision."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:52Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:12:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAD-Fact: A Multi-Agent Debate Framework for Long-Form Factuality Evaluation in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:41:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T07:50:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) raises critical concerns about the factual accuracy of their outputs, especially in high-risk domains such as biomedicine, law, and education. Existing evaluation methods for short texts often fail on long-form content due to complex reasoning chains, intertwined perspectives, and cumulative information. To address this, we propose a systematic approach integrating large-scale long-form datasets, multi-agent verification mechanisms, and weighted evaluation metrics. We construct LongHalluQA, a Chinese long-form factuality dataset; and develop MAD-Fact, a debate-based multi-agent verification system. We introduce a fact importance hierarchy to capture the varying significance of claims in long-form texts. Experiments on two benchmarks show that larger LLMs generally maintain higher factual consistency, while domestic models excel on Chinese content. Our work provides a structured framework for evaluating and enhancing factual reliability in long-form LLM outputs, guiding their safe deployment in sensitive domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The article has been accepted by Frontiers of Computer Science (FCS), with the DOI: {10.1007/s11704-025-51369-x}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:50Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:19:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huifeng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Huifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zile","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Zile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BrowseConf: Confidence-Guided Test-Time Scaling for Web Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T15:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:51:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T16:23:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T01:03:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Confidence in LLMs is a useful indicator of model uncertainty and answer reliability. Existing work mainly focused on single-turn scenarios, while research on confidence in complex multi-turn interactions is limited. In this paper, we investigate whether LLM-based search agents have the ability to communicate their own confidence through verbalized confidence scores after long sequences of actions, a significantly more challenging task compared to outputting confidence in a single interaction. Experimenting on open-source agentic models, we first find that models exhibit much higher task accuracy at high confidence while having near-zero accuracy when confidence is low. Based on this observation, we propose Test-Time Scaling (TTS) methods that use confidence scores to determine answer quality, encourage the model to try again until reaching a satisfactory confidence level. Results show that our proposed methods significantly reduce token consumption while demonstrating competitive performance compared to baseline fixed budget TTS methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:39:34Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:39:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:26:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongru","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinzhe","familyName":"Juan","name":"Juan, Xinzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peihang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhe","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jingzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengdi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alita-G: Self-Evolving Generative Agent for Agent Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T02:00:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been shown to perform better when scaffolded into agents with memory, tools, and feedback. Beyond this, self-evolving agents have emerged, but current work largely limits adaptation to prompt rewriting or failure retries. Therefore, we present ALITA-G, a self-evolution framework that transforms a general-purpose agent into a domain expert by systematically generating, abstracting, and curating Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools. In this framework, a generalist agent executes a curated suite of target-domain tasks and synthesizes candidate MCPs from successful trajectories. These are then abstracted to parameterized primitives and consolidated into an MCP Box. At inference time, ALITA-G performs retrieval-augmented MCP selection with the help of each tool's descriptions and use cases, before executing an agent equipped with the MCP Executor. Across several benchmarks GAIA, PathVQA, and Humanity's Last Exam, ALITA-G attains strong gains while reducing computation costs. On GAIA validation, it achieves 83.03% pass@1 and 89.09% pass@3, establishing a new state-of-the-art result while reducing mean tokens per example by approximately 15% relative to a strong baseline agent. ALITA-G thus provides a principled pathway from generalist capability to reusable, domain-specific competence, improving both accuracy and efficiency on complex reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:43:01Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:43:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:43:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengchao","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Tengchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yupan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Furu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code Aesthetics with Agentic Reward Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:32:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:40:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have become valuable assistants for developers in code-related tasks. While LLMs excel at traditional programming tasks such as code generation and bug fixing, they struggle with visually-oriented coding tasks, often producing suboptimal aesthetics. In this paper, we introduce a new pipeline to enhance the aesthetic quality of LLM-generated code. We first construct AesCode-358K, a large-scale instruction-tuning dataset focused on code aesthetics. Next, we propose agentic reward feedback, a multi-agent system that evaluates executability, static aesthetics, and interactive aesthetics. Building on this, we develop GRPO-AR, which integrates these signals into the GRPO algorithm for joint optimization of functionality and code aesthetics. Finally, we develop OpenDesign, a benchmark for assessing code aesthetics. Experimental results show that combining supervised fine-tuning on AesCode-358K with reinforcement learning using agentic reward feedback significantly improves performance on OpenDesign and also enhances results on existing benchmarks such as PandasPlotBench. Notably, our AesCoder-4B surpasses GPT-4o and GPT-4.1, and achieves performance comparable to large open-source models with 480B-685B parameters, underscoring the effectiveness of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:35:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:35:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:35:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre P","familyName":"Uchoa","name":"Uchoa, Alexandre P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo E T","familyName":"Oliveira","name":"Oliveira, Carlo E T","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudia L R","familyName":"Motta","name":"Motta, Claudia L R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Stakeholder Alignment in LLM-Powered Collaborative AI Systems: A Multi-Agent Framework for Intelligent Tutoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:06:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:39:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models into Intelligent Tutoring Systems pre-sents significant challenges in aligning with diverse and often conflicting values from students, parents, teachers, and institutions. Existing architectures lack for-mal mechanisms for negotiating these multi-stakeholder tensions, creating risks in accountability and bias. This paper introduces the Advisory Governance Layer (AGL), a non-intrusive, multi-agent framework designed to enable distributed stakeholder participation in AI governance. The AGL employs specialized agents representing stakeholder groups to evaluate pedagogical actions against their spe-cific policies in a privacy-preserving manner, anticipating future advances in per-sonal assistant technology that will enhance stakeholder value expression. Through a novel policy taxonomy and conflict-resolution protocols, the frame-work provides structured, auditable governance advice to the ITS without altering its core pedagogical decision-making. This work contributes a reference architec-ture and technical specifications for aligning educational AI with multi-stakeholder values, bridging the gap between high-level ethical principles and practical implementation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:34:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoyeon","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Hoyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byeolhee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byeolhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quality-Aware Translation Tagging in Multilingual RAG system"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T07:11:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:26:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual Retrieval-Augmented Generation (mRAG) often retrieves English documents and translates them into the query language for low-resource settings. However, poor translation quality degrades response generation performance. Existing approaches either assume sufficient translation quality or utilize the rewriting method, which introduces factual distortion and hallucinations. To mitigate these problems, we propose Quality-Aware Translation Tagging in mRAG (QTT-RAG), which explicitly evaluates translation quality along three dimensions-semantic equivalence, grammatical accuracy, and naturalness\u0026amp;fluency-and attach these scores as metadata without altering the original content. We evaluate QTT-RAG against CrossRAG and DKM-RAG as baselines in two open-domain QA benchmarks (XORQA, MKQA) using six instruction-tuned LLMs ranging from 2.4B to 14B parameters, covering two low-resource languages (Korean and Finnish) and one high-resource language (Chinese). QTT-RAG outperforms the baselines by preserving factual integrity while enabling generator models to make informed decisions based on translation reliability. This approach allows for effective usage of cross-lingual documents in low-resource settings with limited native language documents, offering a practical and robust solution across multilingual domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 MRL Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:18Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:30:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen-Che","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Chen-Che","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Cheng","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Yun-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng-Ruei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Teng-Ruei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P1GPT: a multi-agent LLM workflow module for multi-modal financial information analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T05:52:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled multi-agent reasoning systems capable of collaborative decision-making. However, in financial analysis, most frameworks remain narrowly focused on either isolated single-agent predictors or loosely connected analyst ensembles, and they lack a coherent reasoning workflow that unifies diverse data modalities. We introduce P1GPT, a layered multi-agent LLM framework for multi-modal financial information analysis and interpretable trading decision support. Unlike prior systems that emulate trading teams through role simulation, P1GPT implements a structured reasoning pipeline that systematically fuses technical, fundamental, and news-based insights through coordinated agent communication and integration-time synthesis. Backtesting on multi-modal datasets across major U.S. equities demonstrates that P1GPT achieves superior cumulative and risk-adjusted returns, maintains low drawdowns, and provides transparent causal rationales. These findings suggest that structured reasoning workflows, rather than agent role imitation, offer a scalable path toward explainable and trustworthy financial AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:29:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishvesh","familyName":"Bhat","name":"Bhat, Vishvesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omkar","familyName":"Ghugarkar","name":"Ghugarkar, Omkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Generalization in Agentic Tool Calling: CoreThink Agentic Reasoner and MAVEN Dataset"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:14:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generalization across Agentic tool-calling environments remains a key unsolved challenge in developing reliable agentic reasoning systems. While large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong performance on isolated benchmarks, their ability to transfer reasoning strategies and co-ordinate tools across diverse domains is poorly understood. In this work, we conduct a large-scale evaluation of state-of-the-art LLMs on multiple tool-calling benchmarksBFCL v3, TauBench, Tau2Bench, and AceBenchand introduce MAVEN (Math \u0026amp; Physics Adversarial Verification \u0026amp; Evaluation Network), a new out of distribution (OOD) benchmark designed to stress-test multi-step reasoning through explicit verification and adversarial task composition. Our results show that most current models achieve below 50% accuracy on MAVEN, revealing a significant generalization gap across tool-use settings.\n To address this, we present the CoreThink Agentic Reasoner, a framework that augments LLMs with a lightweight symbolic reasoning layer for structured decomposition and adaptive tool orchestration. Without additional training, it generalizes across all benchmarks, achieving state-of-the-art performance with 530% improvements over existing baselines at roughly one-tenth the computational cost."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:11Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:26:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamil","familyName":"Szczepanik","name":"Szczepanik, Kamil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jarosław A.","familyName":"Chudziak","name":"Chudziak, Jarosław A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collaborative LLM Agents for C4 Software Architecture Design Automation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.11; I.2.7; I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T18:43:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:08:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Software architecture design is a fundamental part of creating every software system. Despite its importance, producing a C4 software architecture model, the preferred notation for such architecture, remains manual and time-consuming. We introduce an LLM-based multi-agent system that automates this task by simulating a dialogue between role-specific experts who analyze requirements and generate the Context, Container, and Component views of the C4 model. Quality is assessed with a hybrid evaluation framework: deterministic checks for structural and syntactic integrity and C4 rule consistency, plus semantic and qualitative scoring via an LLM-as-a-Judge approach. Tested on five canonical system briefs, the workflow demonstrates fast C4 model creation, sustains high compilation success, and delivers semantic fidelity. A comparison of four state-of-the-art LLMs shows different strengths relevant to architectural design. This study contributes to automated software architecture design and its evaluation methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted for the upcoming 59th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences (HICSS-59), 2026, Hawaii, USA. The final published version will appear in the official conference proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:35Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilesh","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Nilesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omesh","familyName":"Tickoo","name":"Tickoo, Omesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijaykrishnan","familyName":"Narayanan","name":"Narayanan, Vijaykrishnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Windsock is Dancing: Adaptive Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:03:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MRAG) has emerged as a promising method to generate factual and up-to-date responses of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) by incorporating non-parametric knowledge from external knowledge bases. However, existing MRAG approaches suffer from static retrieval strategies, inflexible modality selection, and suboptimal utilization of retrieved information, leading to three critical challenges: determining when to retrieve, what modality to incorporate, and how to utilize retrieved information effectively. To address these challenges, we introduce Windsock, a query-dependent module making decisions on retrieval necessity and modality selection, effectively reducing computational overhead and improving response quality. Additionally, we propose Dynamic Noise-Resistance (DANCE) Instruction Tuning, an adaptive training strategy that enhances MLLMs' ability to utilize retrieved information while maintaining robustness against noise. Moreover, we adopt a self-assessment approach leveraging knowledge within MLLMs to convert question-answering datasets to MRAG training datasets. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our proposed method significantly improves the generation quality by 17.07% while reducing 8.95% retrieval times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 UniReps Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adhyayan Veer","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Adhyayan Veer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Law","name":"Law, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Ismail","name":"Ismail, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Rohweder","name":"Rohweder, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"O'Brien","name":"O'Brien, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SwiftSolve: A Self-Iterative, Complexity-Aware Multi-Agent Framework for Competitive Programming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T11:05:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:58:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Correctness alone is insufficient: LLM-generated programs frequently satisfy unit tests while violating contest time or memory budgets. We present SwiftSolve, a complexity-aware multi-agent system for competitive programming that couples algorithmic planning with empirical profiling and complexity-guided repair. We frame competitive programming as a software environment where specialized agents act as programmers, each assuming roles such as planning, coding, profiling, and complexity analysis. A Planner proposes an algorithmic sketch; a deterministic Static Pruner filters high-risk plans; a Coder emits ISO C++17; a Profiler compiles and executes candidates on a fixed input-size schedule to record wall time and peak memory; and a Complexity Analyst fits log-log growth (s, R2) with an LLM fallback to assign a complexity class and dispatch targeted patches to either the Planner or Coder. Agents communicate via typed, versioned JSON; a controller enforces iteration caps and diminishing returns stopping. Evaluated on 26 problems (16 BigO, 10 Codeforces Div. 2) in a POSIX sandbox (2 s / 256-512 MB), SwiftSolve attains pass@1 = 61.54% (16/26) on the first attempt and Solved@\u0026lt;=3 = 80.77% with marginal latency change (mean 11.96 s to 12.66 s per attempt). Aggregate run-level success is 73.08% at 12.40 s mean. Failures are predominantly resource-bound, indicating inefficiency rather than logic errors. Against Claude Opus 4, SwiftSolve improves run-level success (73.1% vs 52.6%) at approximately 2x runtime overhead (12.4 s vs 6.8 s). Beyond correctness (pass@k), we report efficiency metrics (eff@k for runtime and memory, incidence of TLE or MLE, and complexity fit accuracy on BigO), demonstrating that profiling and complexity-guided replanning reduce inefficiency while preserving accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:19:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22559","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22559","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22559","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Chunyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Closed-Loop Personalized Learning Agent Integrating Neural Cognitive Diagnosis, Bounded-Ability Adaptive Testing, and LLM-Driven Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T07:32:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As information technology advances, education is moving from one-size-fits-all instruction toward personalized learning. However, most methods handle modeling, item selection, and feedback in isolation rather than as a closed loop. This leads to coarse or opaque student models, assumption-bound adaptivity that ignores diagnostic posteriors, and generic, non-actionable feedback. To address these limitations, this paper presents an end-to-end personalized learning agent, EduLoop-Agent, which integrates a Neural Cognitive Diagnosis model (NCD), a Bounded-Ability Estimation Computerized Adaptive Testing strategy (BECAT), and large language models (LLMs). The NCD module provides fine-grained estimates of students' mastery at the knowledge-point level; BECAT dynamically selects subsequent items to maximize relevance and learning efficiency; and LLMs convert diagnostic signals into structured, actionable feedback. Together, these components form a closed-loop framework of ``Diagnosis--Recommendation--Feedback.'' Experiments on the ASSISTments dataset show that the NCD module achieves strong performance on response prediction while yielding interpretable mastery assessments. The adaptive recommendation strategy improves item relevance and personalization, and the LLM-based feedback offers targeted study guidance aligned with identified weaknesses. Overall, the results indicate that the proposed design is effective and practically deployable, providing a feasible pathway to generating individualized learning trajectories in intelligent education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22559","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:18:12Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:18:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:18:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Kaitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijia","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Backward-Friendly Optimization: Training Large Language Models with Approximate Gradients under Memory Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:46:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Full fine-tuning of Large Language Models (LLMs) is notoriously memory-intensive, primarily because conventional optimizers such as SGD or Adam assume access to exact gradients derived from cached activations. Existing solutions either alter the model architecture (e.g., reversible networks) or trade memory for computation (e.g., activation checkpointing), but the optimizer itself remains untouched. In this work, we introduce GradLite, a backward-friendly optimizer that relaxes the requirement of exact gradients, enabling efficient training even when intermediate activations are aggressively discarded or approximated. GradLite leverages two key techniques: (i) low-rank Jacobian approximation, which reduces the dimensionality of backpropagated error signals, and (ii) error-feedback correction, which accumulates and compensates approximation errors across iterations to preserve convergence guarantees. We provide a theoretical analysis showing that GradLite maintains unbiased gradient estimates with bounded variance, ensuring convergence rates comparable to Adam. Empirically, GradLite reduces optimizer-state and activation memory consumption by up to 50\\% without architectural changes, and achieves on-par or superior downstream performance on reasoning (MMLU, GSM8K), multilingual, and dialogue benchmarks compared to checkpointing and optimizer-centric baselines (LoMo, GaLore)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Veerabadran","name":"Veerabadran, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanyi","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitin","familyName":"Kamra","name":"Kamra, Nitin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Matias","name":"Matias, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caley","familyName":"Drooff","name":"Drooff, Caley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett D","familyName":"Roads","name":"Roads, Brett D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riley","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Riley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Carlberg","name":"Carlberg, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Tighe","name":"Tighe, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Ridgeway","name":"Ridgeway, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Egocentric Multimodal Goal Inference for Assistive Wearable Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T21:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:44:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There has been a surge of interest in assistive wearable agents: agents embodied in wearable form factors (e.g., smart glasses) who take assistive actions toward a user's goal/query (e.g. \"Where did I leave my keys?\"). In this work, we consider the important complementary problem of inferring that goal from multi-modal contextual observations. Solving this \"goal inference\" problem holds the promise of eliminating the effort needed to interact with such an agent. This work focuses on creating WAGIBench, a strong benchmark to measure progress in solving this problem using vision-language models (VLMs). Given the limited prior work in this area, we collected a novel dataset comprising 29 hours of multimodal data from 348 participants across 3,477 recordings, featuring ground-truth goals alongside accompanying visual, audio, digital, and longitudinal contextual observations. We validate that human performance exceeds model performance, achieving 93% multiple-choice accuracy compared with 84% for the best-performing VLM. Generative benchmark results that evaluate several families of modern vision-language models show that larger models perform significantly better on the task, yet remain far from practical usefulness, as they produce relevant goals only 55% of the time. Through a modality ablation, we show that models benefit from extra information in relevant modalities with minimal performance degradation from irrelevant modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a spotlight paper at the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22344","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22344","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22344","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Aghajani","familyName":"Asl","name":"Asl, Mohammad Aghajani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Asgari-Bidhendi","name":"Asgari-Bidhendi, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behrooz","familyName":"Minaei-Bidgoli","name":"Minaei-Bidgoli, Behrooz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FAIR-RAG: Faithful Adaptive Iterative Refinement for Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50, 68P20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T15:59:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:39:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates hallucination and knowledge staleness in Large Language Models (LLMs), existing frameworks often falter on complex, multi-hop queries that require synthesizing information from disparate sources. Current advanced RAG methods, employing iterative or adaptive strategies, lack a robust mechanism to systematically identify and fill evidence gaps, often propagating noise or failing to gather a comprehensive context. We introduce FAIR-RAG, a novel agentic framework that transforms the standard RAG pipeline into a dynamic, evidence-driven reasoning process. At its core is an Iterative Refinement Cycle governed by a module we term Structured Evidence Assessment (SEA). The SEA acts as an analytical gating mechanism: it deconstructs the initial query into a checklist of required findings and audits the aggregated evidence to identify confirmed facts and, critically, explicit informational gaps. These gaps provide a precise signal to an Adaptive Query Refinement agent, which generates new, targeted sub-queries to retrieve missing information. This cycle repeats until the evidence is verified as sufficient, ensuring a comprehensive context for a final, strictly faithful generation. We conducted experiments on challenging multi-hop QA benchmarks, including HotpotQA, 2WikiMultiHopQA, and MusiQue. In a unified experimental setup, FAIR-RAG significantly outperforms strong baselines. On HotpotQA, it achieves an F1-score of 0.453 -- an absolute improvement of 8.3 points over the strongest iterative baseline -- establishing a new state-of-the-art for this class of methods on these benchmarks. Our work demonstrates that a structured, evidence-driven refinement process with explicit gap analysis is crucial for unlocking reliable and accurate reasoning in advanced RAG systems for complex, knowledge-intensive tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 5 figures, 5 tables. Keywords: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Large Language Models (LLMs), Agentic AI, Multi-hop Question Answering, Faithfulness"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22344","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CGoT: A Novel Inference Mechanism for Embodied Multi-Agent Systems Using Composable Graphs of Thoughts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T09:39:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:32:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of self-driving cars and service robots is becoming increasingly prevalent across a wide array of fields, playing a crucial and expanding role in both industrial applications and everyday life. In parallel, the rapid advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have garnered substantial attention and interest within the research community. This paper introduces a novel vehicle-robot system that leverages the strengths of both autonomous vehicles and service robots. In our proposed system, two autonomous ego-vehicles transports service robots to locations within an office park, where they perform a series of tasks. The study explores the feasibility and potential benefits of incorporating LLMs into this system, with the aim of enhancing operational efficiency and maximizing the potential of the cooperative mechanisms between the vehicles and the robots. This paper proposes a novel inference mechanism which is called CGOT toward this type of system where an agent can carry another agent. Experimental results are presented to validate the performance of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:10:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:10:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:10:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinni","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangning","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry Peng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Henry Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Embracing Trustworthy Brain-Agent Collaboration as Paradigm Extension for Intelligent Assistive Technologies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T00:25:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:19:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Brain-Computer Interfaces (BCIs) offer a direct communication pathway between the human brain and external devices, holding significant promise for individuals with severe neurological impairments. However, their widespread adoption is hindered by critical limitations, such as low information transfer rates and extensive user-specific calibration. To overcome these challenges, recent research has explored the integration of Large Language Models (LLMs), extending the focus from simple command decoding to understanding complex cognitive states. Despite these advancements, deploying agentic AI faces technical hurdles and ethical concerns. Due to the lack of comprehensive discussion on this emerging direction, this position paper argues that the field is poised for a paradigm extension from BCI to Brain-Agent Collaboration (BAC). We emphasize reframing agents as active and collaborative partners for intelligent assistance rather than passive brain signal data processors, demanding a focus on ethical data handling, model reliability, and a robust human-agent collaboration framework to ensure these systems are safe, trustworthy, and effective."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS'25 Position Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:07:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:07:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:07:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FeaGPT: an End-to-End agentic-AI for Finite Element Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:55:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:11:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are establishing new paradigms for engineering applications by enabling natural language control of complex computational workflows. This paper introduces FeaGPT, the first framework to achieve complete geometry-mesh-simulation workflows through conversational interfaces. Unlike existing tools that automate individual FEA components, FeaGPT implements a fully integrated Geometry-Mesh-Simulation-Analysis (GMSA) pipeline that transforms engineering specifications into validated computational results without manual intervention. The system interprets engineering intent, automatically generates physics-aware adaptive meshes, configures complete FEA simulations with proper boundary condition inference, and performs multi-objective analysis through closed-loop iteration.\n Experimental validation confirms complete end-to-end automation capability. Industrial turbocharger cases (7-blade compressor and 12-blade turbine at \\SI{110000}{rpm}) demonstrate the system successfully transforms natural language specifications into validated CalculiX simulations, producing physically realistic results for rotating machinery analysis. Additional validation through 432 NACA airfoil configurations confirms scalability for parametric design exploration. These results demonstrate that natural language interfaces can effectively democratize access to advanced computational engineering tools while preserving analytical rigor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:46Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:04:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":36,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+RAG+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+memory%29+AND+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22%29%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}